These stylesheets theme MediaWiki core's jquery.ui ResourceLoader module, which core marks
deprecated in favour of Codex: resources/Resources.php declares it with 'deprecated' => 'Please
use Codex instead.'. They exist only to keep the screens that still instantiate jQuery UI
widgets from breaking under the Notion palette.

Vector records upstream that in November 2022, after a discussion between the Growth, Editing
and Web teams (and others), it was agreed that these styles should be considered frozen and not
modified further. That is Wikimedia's decision rather than this skin's, and it is inherited
here with the files. More context:
https://www.mediawiki.org/wiki/Readers/Web/Dev_notes/jQuery_UI

The rule for Notion is therefore RETHEME, DO NOT REFACTOR. Permitted: recolouring declarations
to the Notion palette; replacing gradient background-image chrome with a flat background-color;
reducing the icon sprite set kept in images/; and, where a recolour would otherwise leave a
control invisible or unpositioned, adding an explicit declaration for it. Record every such
deviation in PATCHES.

Not permitted: restructuring or "modernising" selectors; renaming any .ui-* class (jQuery UI's
own JavaScript applies those names at runtime, so a rename silently unstyles the widget);
rewriting these files against a newer upstream release; deleting the deliberate legacy hacks --
zoom: 1, the IE filter:Alpha(Opacity=...) declarations, the ::-moz-focus-inner resets, the IE6
.ui-datepicker-cover iframe fix, the vendor-prefixed border-radius properties; reformatting
wholesale for style.

Note what "colour, not structure" does and does not mean. It is the rule for how to EDIT these
files, not a claim that a diff against Vector's copy contains nothing but colour values. It does
not, and PATCHES enumerates the exceptions: at least one rule set here (.ui-dialog positioning)
is a local addition with no counterpart in upstream ThemeRoller output, the images/ directory
holds a deliberately smaller and differently named sprite set than Vector's, and recolouring an
overlay tone by dropping its image and declaring a flat colour changes what a reader sees, not
merely which hex is written down. Those are sanctioned deviations, recorded rather than hidden.
What is forbidden is adding NEW structural change without recording it.

The stakes here are unusually high. Core's jquery.ui module supplies scripts, languageScripts
and a skinStyles.default array of the bundled smoothness theme, and this skin lists its
jquery.ui entry in ResourceModuleSkinStyles."notion" as an array with no leading "+", which
replaces skinStyles.default instead of appending to it (FileModule::setSkinStylesOverride()).
Those 14 stylesheets are therefore the whole jQuery UI theme under Notion: a missing or
mistyped filename leaves its widget with no CSS at all rather than merely the wrong colours.

STATUS, so that no one reads the paragraph above as a completeness claim. skin.json declares all
14. Two exist in this folder today -- jquery.ui.core.css and jquery.ui.theme.css -- and the
remaining twelve (accordion, autocomplete, button, datepicker, dialog, menu, progressbar,
resizable, selectable, slider, tabs, tooltip) are still to be authored. Until they are, the
widgets they cover have no CSS under this skin, which is precisely the failure mode described
above rather than a hypothetical one. Treat the 14-file list as the target and this note as the
record of how far it has been met; ResourceLoader tolerates the gap silently, so nothing else
will tell you.

Read PATCHES, in this folder, before editing anything here. It records the per-file deviations
from upstream jQuery UI 1.9.2 (and from 1.11.2-alpha, the release the button stylesheet was
taken from) that must be preserved, among them the @noflip annotations that stop CSSJanus
flipping the datepicker and resizable rules and the RTL dialog-placement fix at
https://phabricator.wikimedia.org/T344467.

This file is documentation for maintainers: it is not referenced from skin.json and is never
served by ResourceLoader, so it must not be added to the jquery.ui array.
